## 1. Introduction
Excessive consumption of fossil fuels has exacerbated the environmental and energy crisis [1]. Therefore, it is highly desirable to develop eco-friendly devices for the conversion and storage of clean and sustainable energies [2]. Metal-air batteries and water-splitting devices have received considerable attention in recent years due to their high efficiency, low cost, and environmental friendliness [3–6]. The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is a core reaction for metal-air batteries and water splitting devices [7,8]. However, OER is typically involved in four electron-proton coupled reactions, which leads to its sluggish reaction kinetics. As a result, the majority of catalysts require a high potential to drive OER [9–12]. Although $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ are the most efficient electrocatalysts for OER however their scarcity, high cost, and poor long-term stability impede their commercial utilization on a large-scale [13–15].
Due to their high internal surface area, intrinsic benefits of high porosity, and uniformly dispersed metal atoms, metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) synthesized from metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands via coordination bonds for the conversion and storage of energy have been extensively researched [16,17]. Various MOFs, in particular, have been reported to catalyze OER in alkaline media [18–21]. To maximize the advantages of the MOFs, they should be directly used them as (pre)electrocatalysts without any post-treatment. However, most of the MOFs exhibit suboptimal catalytic activity, owing predominantly to their intrinsically poor charge/mass transport capabilities [22– 25]. Reducing the size of MOFs to the nanoscale level is an effective approach for overcoming the aforementioned drawbacks [26–29]. However, the majority of the nanosized MOFs reported to date have been synthesized in powder form, necessitating the need for a polymer binder to prepare the MOF-based electrode. The polymer binder not only reduces the number of exposed active sites of the MOFs but also the electrode’s overall electrical conductivity of the electrode, resulting in suboptimal OER activity [30–39]. In-situ growth of MOFs on conductive substrates such as carbon fiber cloth (CC) and nickel foam (NF) is one of the viable strategies for addressing the aforesaid problem [40,41]. The nanosized MOFs on the conductive substrates typically exhibited three-dimensionally (3D) interconnected open morphologies, which not only enhanced the electrode’s electrical conductivity but also its mass transport characteristic. Consequently, the nanosized MOFs on the conductive substrates demonstrated considerably higher OER activity than the substrate-free counterparts. However, it is difficult to control the intrinsic properties such as adsorption/desorption energy of intermediates on the active sites just relying on anchoring nanosized MOFs on the conductive substrates.
Recently, interface engineering has been demonstrated to be a potential strategy for enhancing electrocatalytic activity [42]. The interfaces between different components could modulate the electronic structures of the heterocatalysts and change the local electron density, and thereby could improve the intrinsic activity of the heterocatalysts. The interfaces between different components may change the local electron density modulate the electronic structures of the heterocatalysts, thereby improving the intrinsic activity of the heterocatalysts. Many heterocatalysts with well-defined interfaces, including NiTe/NiS, $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}S_{4}/F e O O H}$ and $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{N i}{-}\mathsf{N i}0$ heterointerfaces, have been developed for various catalytic reactions [43,44]. However, there are few reports on the fabrication of MOFs-based heterostructures on conductive substrates so far. Meanwhile, the OER performance and catalytic mechanism of MOFs-based heterostructures have not been thoroughly described.
In this paper, we employ a coordination-assisted solventthermal technique to covalently bond nickel-based FeNi-MOF nanorods (NRs) to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ nanosheets (NSs) for high-efficiency water oxidation. Due to the in-situ growth of the heterocatalyst on the conductive CC substrate, the heterocatalyst exhibits a 3D interconnected open morphology, which facilitates the charge/- mass transport during OER which facilitates the charge/mass transport during OER. We observe that the bridging agent caffeic acid (CA) is crucial for the in-situ growth of the FeNi-MOF NRs on the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}\mathsf{N S s}$ . Notably, CA can operate as a charge transfer channel, facilitating the electron transfer from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ . The charge transfer modifies the electronic structure of the FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalyst, and enhances the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ intermediates on MOFs, leading to improved OER activity of the heterocatalyst. As a result, the optimal FeNi-MOF/ $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterostructure outperforms the benchmark $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ catalyst in regards to OER, with the overpotential of only $256~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Material synthesis
Synthesis of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 NRs. The $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NSs and the Mo-precursor NW/CC $(1\times4\:\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ were synthesized using our previously designed method [45], whereas the FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}{-}2}$ was prepared using a reported procedure with slight changes [46]. $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NSs (mass loading is $2.0\mathrm{\mg\cm}^{-2}.$ ) was first immersed in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol containing $25~\mathrm{mg}$ CA for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ prior to the experiment. Following that, $133~\mathrm{mg}$ terephthalic acid (TPA), $86.4~\mathrm{mg}$ iron (III) chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0);$ , and $92.6~\mathrm{mg}$ nickel nitrate hexahydrate (Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ were dissolved in 20 mL N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF). The suspension was mixed and ultrasonicated until it was completely homogeneous. The above solution was subsequently transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave containing $\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ $(1\times4\mathrm{cm}^{2}\cdot$ solution which was then sealed and maintained at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the FeNi-MOF $/C\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}.$ 2 was thoroughly washed with ethanol and DMF, vacuum dried at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight, and designated as FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2.
The concentrations of TPA $\mathrm{(67~mg)}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(43.2~\mathrm{mg})$ ), and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $(46.3~\mathrm{mg})$ were optimized to investigate the effect of MOF content on the catalytic performance of the catalyst toward the OER, and the corresponding product was labeled FeNi-MOF ${}^{I}\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}.$ 1. Simultaneously, the concentrations of TPA $\left(266{\mathrm{~mg}}\right)$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ ( $172.8{\mathrm{~mg}}{\cdot}$ , and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ ( $185.2~\mathrm{mg},$ were also optimized, and the product was designated FeNi-MOF $/{\mathsf{C A}}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–3$ . The mass loading for all FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1, FeNi-M $\mathsf{I O F/C A/N i M o O}_{4}–2$ , and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 on CC was approximately $1.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ .
Synthesis of FeNi-MOF/CC. FeNi-MOF was prepared under the identical solvothermal conditions as that of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ , except the absence of $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ . The FeNiMOF was first ultrasonically dispersed in a solution comprising $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.7\ \mathrm{\mL})$ , ethanol $(0.2\ \mathrm{\mL})$ , and a $5\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ Nafion solution $(0.1~\mathrm{mL})$ ) for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Then, $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was dropped on the CC with a surface area of $1.0\times1.0\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and dried at room temperature. The mass loading of FeNi-MOF was about $1.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
Synthesis of the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm C{\mathsf C}$ . To prepare $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/\mathrm C{\mathsf C}$ electrodes, $5\mathrm{mg}$ of commercial $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ was dispersed in $450~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol, followed by the addition of $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ Nafion solution. A homogenous catalyst ink was formed after at least $30\mathrm{min}$ of ultra-sonication, which was then coated onto a CC with a mass loading of $3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 2.2. Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical measurements were performed in a typical three-electrode system with a graphite rod as the counter electrode and an $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode (KCl saturated) as the reference electrode using a CHI 660E electrochemical analyzer (CH Instruments, Inc., Shanghai). The self-supported electrode with a geometric area of $0.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ was employed as the working electrode. The electrolytes for all the electrochemical tests were bubbled with high-purity $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ for at least $30\ \mathrm{min}$ before the measurements to remove dissolved oxygen in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH, and the electrochemical data was collected until a stable current density was maintained by CV measurements. Polarization curves were acquired using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with $85\%$ iR correction and a scan rate of $1\mathrm{~mV~}s^{-1}$ . CV and chronopotentiometry techniques were used to evaluate the stability. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH over a frequency range of $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ . All measured potentials were calibrated to RHE using the equation $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}+0.21\ \mathrm{V}$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}+\ (0.059\ \nabla\times\ p\mathrm{H})$ , while the current densities were corrected against Ohmic potential drop. The electrodes were stabilized through the CV before the data collection.
Further detailed information on the reagents, materials characterizations, turnover frequency (TOF) calculation, theoretical calculations, and additional results are provided in the Supporting Information.
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthesis of the heterocatalysts based on FeNi-MOF NRs and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ NSs employing CA as a bridging agent (denoted as FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4) is illustrated in Scheme 1. The Moprecursor NWs and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ NSs were synthesized using our previously reported methods and displayed similar morphologies and crystalline structures to those we prepared previously (Fig. S1) [47]. The $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs were soaked in a CA solution to graft the CA bridging agent (See the Experimental Section for details). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern demonstrates that the modification of CA does not affect the crystalline structure of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs (Fig. S2a). Magnified scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images reveal that $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs remain anchored to the CC after the CA modification, demonstrating that the 3D structure is effectively preserved and CA bridging does not alter the morphology of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs (Fig. S2b). Fourier transform infrared (FT–IR) and UV–vis spectra of CA-treated $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs $(\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ NRs) were performed to validate the surface modification of $\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}@.$ $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs with CA. The $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ NRs were washed with ethanol several times before measurements to remove the CA that had been absorbed physically at the surface. For comparison, the FT–IR, and UV–vis spectra of CA and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs were also measured. As illustrated in Fig. S3a, for pure CA, the peak at $1670~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is due to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ absorption, whereas the peaks at $950~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3200{-}3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the out-of-plane bending vibration of carboxyl $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ and outward curvature vibration of adjacent hydroxyl, respectively [48]. The bands around $456~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ , $966~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $645~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the IR spectrum of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ are attributed to MoAOAMo stretching vibration, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mo}=0}$ stretching vibration, and an in-plane ${\mathsf{N i}}{\mathsf{-O-H}}$ bending vibration, respectively [49]. For the $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ NRs, the signals at 950, 1670, and $3400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ that originate from CA, as well as those at 456, 966, and $645~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , emanate from $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ heterostructure are clearly observed, demonstrating that the CA is successfully bonded to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ . Furthermore, UV–Vis spectra reveal that the absorption intensity of $\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ is increased, and its absorption spectral region is extended to the infrared region, as compared to CA and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ (Fig. S3b). The result indicates that there is strong electronic interaction at the interfaces between $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@$ - $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ and CA, further confirming that CA is successfully bonded to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs [50]. Finally, the heterocatalysts were obtained by solvothermal treatment of the $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ in DMF solution containing TPA, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , and ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ (Scheme 1). To investigate the influence of FeNi-MOF content on catalytic activity, three heterocatalysts were synthesized, and designated as FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1, FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O}_{4}–2$ , and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 (see the Experimental Section for details).
Fig. 1a depicts the XRD patterns of the as-fabricated heterocatalysts. There are distinct diffraction peaks located at $2\theta$ in the range of $8\mathrm{-}11^{\circ}$ and $16{-}22^{\circ}$ which match well to those of bulk FeNi-MOF [51] along with the NiMoO4 and CC peaks. Thus, the XRD results indicate that the FeNi-MOF is successfully produced. Remarkably, the intensities of the diffraction peaks of FeNi-MOF in FeNi-MOF/ $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}-}$ 1, $\mathrm{FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO_{4}{-}2},$ and FeNi-MOF ${}^{I}\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}\mathrm{-}$ 3 are gradually increased in accordance with the amount of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ precursors. The mass ratios of FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1, FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ -2, and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 are 5.0:1, 7.4:1, and 9.8:1 (Table S1), respectively according to the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometers (ICP-OES) which are consistent with the XRD results. Notably, the diffraction peaks of the Ni $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{\cdot}0.75\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ that existed in the precursorvanish, implying that they participate in the reactions for the growth of FeNi-MOF. The results demonstrate that our heterocatalysts with a tunable ratio of FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ can be prepared by adjusting the amounts of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ . SEM images of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}.$ -1, FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2, and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 are illustrated in Fig. 1b-d. The hierarchical $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ NRs transform into irregular soil-like structures with spindle-like FeNi-MOF NRs sprouting from the surfaces of the irregular $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ structures, resembling plants growing in the soil. The heterocatalysts for OER exhibit robust stability because of such structure. The FeNiMOF in FeNi- $\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O}_{4^{-1}}$ has a length and diameter of $2.1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ respectively. The sizes of the FeNi-MOF dramatically decrease with the increase of the amount of the $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ ·6- $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ . Typically, the length and diameter of FeNi-MOF reduce to $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $370~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4- 2, and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $250~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3. However, as illustrated in Fig. 1b-d, the density of the FeNi-MOF increases as the amount of the $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ increases. The density of FeNi-MOF in the FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathrm{\DeltaNiMoO_{4}-j}$ 3 is so high that most of the FeNi-MOF NRs reside flat on the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ soil surfaces. Therefore, in comparison FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1 and FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O}_{4}–2$ , $\mathrm{\bar{\eNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO_{4}-3}}$ does not exhibit 3D open structures. Remarkably, sparse FeNi-MOF NRs are developed on the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ soil when the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs are not treated in the CA solution, as shown Fig. S4, demonstrating that the CA bridging agent is critical for the growth of FeNi-MOF on the surface of the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ . This phenomenon indicates metal-ligand coordination between the adjacent hydroxyl groups of CA and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , verifying the successful bonding of CA to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed for the formation energies $(E_{\mathrm{form}})$ of FeNi-MOF on the CA bonded $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ and the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ without CA treatment to understand the role of the CA bridging agent. Fig. 2 depicts the structure models for FeNi-MO $\mathrm{F/CA/NiMoO_{4}}$ and FeNi-MOF/NiMoO4. The C atom of the MOFs is bonded to the carboxyl group O of CA in the FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ model, while the two hydroxyl O atoms of CA are bonded to the adjacent Ni atoms of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ (Fig. 2a). Two C atoms of the MOFs are bonded to the adjacent Ni and Mo atoms of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ , in the FeNi-MOF $/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ model, respectively (Fig. 2b). The calculated results show that the $E_{\mathrm{form}}$ of FeNi-MOF/ $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ is $-1.14\ \mathrm{{\eV}}$ , indicating that the formation of FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalysts is thermodynamically favorable [52]. In contrast, the $E_{\mathrm{form}}$ of the FeNi-MOF/NiMoO4 model is significantly larger than zero (Fig. 2c). Furthermore, the $E_{\mathrm{form}}$ values of other FeNi-MOF $/\mathrm{NiMoO_{4}}$ models are also positive (Fig. S5). Thus, in the absence of the CA bridging agent, the synthesis of the FeNi-MOF $/\mathrm{NiMoO_{4}}$ is not possible thermodynamically. The presence of the spare FeNi-MOF NRs in the sample prepared using $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NRs as precursors without CA treatment is related to the presence of the $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ NSs that can provide the Ni sources for FeNi-MOF growth. To verify this point, we developed the FeNi-MOF using pure $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ nanowires as precursors to grow the FeNi-MOF. As illustrated in Fig. S6, FeNi-MOF there are no spindles on the surfaces of the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ nanowires confirming that the CA bridging agent is crucial to develop the FeNi-MOF $/\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}$ heterocatalyst.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was used to examine the structure of the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 heterocatalysts. Fig. 3a illustrates a low-magnification TEM image and the energydispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray (EDX) elemental mappings of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–2$ . The TEM image is divided into two regions, the central area of which is denoted by the pink puppy. The Mo, Ni, and O signals regions are observed clearly in the pink puppy, indicating the central region is composed of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ . Meanwhile, the Fe, Ni, C, and O elements are uniformly distributed throughout the outer region, implying that the outer region is mainly composed of the FeNi-MOF NRs. The elemental mappings by TEM and EDX further demonstrate that the FeNi-MOF NRs are successfully grafted on the irregular $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ soil via CA bridging agent. Fig. 3b depicts a TEM image of an individual FeNi-MOF NRs at the outer surface of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ . The NR exhibits a length and diameter of $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and
$375~\mathrm{{nm}}$ which are consistent with the SEM observations. EDX elemental mappings confirm that the NR is bimetallic FeNi-MOF (Fig. 3b). Mo signals surrounding the FeNi-MOF NR are observed, confirming the FeNi-MOF NRs’ strong coupling with $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ in the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 heterocatalysts.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy has been utilized to investigate the surface compositions and the valence states of the elements in FeNi-MOF, $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ , and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2. Fig. 4a displays the Fe 2p XPS spectra of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 and FeNi-MOF, with two peaks at 711.91 and $726.35\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , respectively [53]. In comparison to FeNi-MOF, the peak positions of Fe 2p in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 are shifted to higher binding energies. Similarly, the binding energies of the ${\mathsf{N i}}2{\mathsf{p}}$ in FeNi-MOF/ $\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}\mathrm{-}2$ (Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ 856.18 eV, Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}874.46\mathsf{e V})$ are also positively shifted, when compared to those of the FeNi-MOF (Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $855.79\ \mathrm{eV}$ , Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}873.41\ \mathrm{eV})$ , as demonstrated in Fig. 4b [54]. Therefore, the strong electronic interactions between FeNi-MOF and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ occur in the heterocatalysts, and the electrons in the FeNi-MOF are extracted upon coupling with $\mathrm{CA}/\mathrm{NiMoO}_{4}@\mathrm{Ni}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . In contrast, the binding energies of the Ni 2p are positively shifted in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 (Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ 856.18 eV, Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ $874.46\ \mathrm{eV};$ , when compared to those of $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ (Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}855.43\mathrm{eV}$ , Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}873.36\mathrm{eV};$ , indicating that the electrons in the FeNi-MOF are extracted upon coupling with $\mathrm{CA/NiMoO}_{4}@$ - $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ (Fig. S7a). Furthermore, the binding energies of the ${\mathsf{N i}}\ 2{\mathsf{p}}$ are negatively shifted in $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ compared to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ (Ni 2p3/2 856.34 eV, Ni 2p1/2 873.81 eV), revealing that the electron density of the $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ is enhanced after the CA treatment (Fig. S7a). The CA bridging agent can transport the electrons from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ as demonstrated in the preceding discussion. Fig. S7b compares the Mo 3d XPS spectra of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2, $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ . According to Mo 3d, XPS spectra [55] ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{4+}$ and ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{6+}$ species are detected in these samples. However, when the binding energies of $\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ are compared to those of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ , the Mo 3d binding energies in FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 not only shift negatively but also the concentration of ${\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{o}}^{4+}$ increases, demonstrating that the electrons transfer from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ in the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 heterocatalyst [46]. Furthermore, the C 1s spectrum of FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}{-}2}$ displays three peaks centered at 284.8, 286.3, and $288.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}},{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0$ , and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}0$ , respectively (Fig. S7c) [49]. The O 1s spectrum of the FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}.$ -2 is illustrated in Fig. S6d with the peaks at 530.3, 531.6, and $532.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ assigned to metal-oxygen bond, the O atoms bonded to metals, and the absorbed water molecules, respectively [56]. In addition, the surface compositions of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1 and
FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 are identical to those of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–2$ (Fig. S8 and Fig. S9), indicating that the strong electronic interactions between FeNi-MOF and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ also occur in both samples.
XPS results demonstrate that the FeNi- $\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalysts have strong electronic interactions. DFT calculations for the charge density difference of the FeNi-M $\mathsf{I O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalysts were performed based on the model in Fig. 2a to acquire a deeper understanding of the charge redistributions near the CA interface. Fig. 4c the calculated charge density difference of the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 heterocatalysts, is shown in Fig. 4c with yellow and cyan representing electron accumulation and electron depletion, respectively. As illustrated in Fig. 4d, the side of the CA bonded to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ is electron-rich, whereas the side bonded to FeNi-MOF is electron-deficient, confirming that the electrons transfer from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ via the CA bridging agent. The CA can modify the electronic structure of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ according to the theoretical calculations and XPS analyses. These electronic interactions at the interfaces can enhance the interactions of reactants and/or intermediates with the catalyst surface, hence improving the heterocatalysts’ catalytic activities. The density of states (DOS) of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4, $\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ , FeNi-MOF, and CA near the Fermi level were also calculated to investigate the influence of the CA bridging agent on the electrical conductivities of these samples. The DFT calculations indicate that FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 has significantly enhanced DOS near the Fermi level (black dotted line in Fig. 4e), as compared to that of the other reference samples. Therefore, coupling FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ via the CA bridging agent improves the heterocatalyst’s electrical conductivity, which is favorable of charge transfer during OER.
The OER activities of our self-supported heterocatalysts, namely FeNi-MOF, $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , and FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalysts were evaluated using a conventional three-electrode setup in $0_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH solution.
The OER performances of the FeNi-MOF and the benchmark $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ loaded on the CC substrate with the same loading mass as the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 were also measured under identical conditions. Prior to OER assessment, cyclic voltammetry (CV) scan was performed to activate the catalyst to obtain optimized OER performance. LSV was recorded at $1\mathrm{~mV~}\boldsymbol{s}^{-1}$ with an iR correction of $85\%$ . Notably, the CC exhibits poor OER activity with a current density of $10~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a large overpotential of $440~\mathrm{\mV}$ (Fig. S10). As depicted in Fig. 5a, the LSV curve of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ displays a broad oxidation peak in the range of $1.35{-}1.57\mathrm{~V~}$ prior to oxygen evolution, which is attributed to the transition of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ . The oxidation peak disappears after CA treatment, owing to the electronic interaction between CA and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ . The electrons can be transferred from CA to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , as demonstrated by XPS results (Fig. S7a). The transformation of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ is significantly suppressed by such electron transport, resulting in the disappearance of the oxidation peak. Meanwhile, electron-rich $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ in ${\mathsf{C A}}/{\mathsf{N i M o O}}_{4}@\qquad$ - $\mathsf{N i}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ is not conducive with the adsorption of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ intermediates, thereby lowering the OER activity of $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ (Fig. 6a). For instance, at an overpotential of $400~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , the current density achieved by $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ is $66\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ ; while, at the same potential, the current density by $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ decreases to $42\ m\mathrm{A\cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 5a). FeNi-MOF exhibits a current density of $82\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $400~\mathrm{{mV}}$ overpotential, which is substantially higher than of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ , implying that FeNi-MOF could be an active phase in the FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalysts. Remarkably, as coupled with $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the CA bridging agent, the OER activity of FeNiMOF is increased significantly. FeNi-MOF/CA $/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}.$ -1, FeNi$\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}{-}2}$ , and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 require overpotentials of 333, 304, and $343~\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively, to achieve a current density of $82\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which are 62, 116, and $77~\mathrm{mV}$ lower than that of FeNi-MOF. The Tafel equation was used to evaluate the electrocatalytic kinetics of the electrocatalysts toward the OER to shed light on the effect of the interfaces on their OER kinetics. The Tafel plots in Fig. 5b demonstrate that the Tafel slopes of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ -1, FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2, and FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-3 are 77.6, 68.9, and $78.3~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are significantly smaller than those of FeNi-MOF $(80.5~\mathrm{\mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ $(348.8\quad{\mathrm{mV}}\quad{\mathrm{dec}}^{-1})$ and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ $(351.8~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}.$ . Therefore, in terms of the overpotentials and the Tafel slopes, the OER activity of the catalysts follows in order $\mathrm{\DeltaCA/NiMoO_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}<N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}<\Delta}$ FeNi-MOF $\angle\cdot\angle$ FeNi-MO $\mathrm{F/CA/NiMoO_{4}{-}3\mathrm{\leftarrowFeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO_{4}{-}2.}}$ . Moreover, FeNi-MOF/ $\mathrm{\partial_{\cdot}A/N i M o O_{4}-}$ 2 exhibits the highest OER activity among these FeNi-M $\mathrm{2F/CA/NiMoO_{4}}$ heterocatalysts. In addition, FeNi-MOF/CA/ $N i\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{-2}$ exhibits greater OER activity in a 1.0 M KOH solution than most of the other MOFs-based OER electrocatalysts (Fig. 5c). Therefore, the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 is the most promising catalyst for OER.
According to XPS analyses, electrons in the FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ heterocatalysts can be transferred from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the CA bridge, which regulates the electronic structure of FeNi-MOF. The electron transfer renders FeNi-MOF electron-deficient facilitating the adsorption of the $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ intermediates and thereby enhancing the OER activity of FeNi-MOF (Fig. 6b). Meanwhile, DFT calculations indicate that coupling FeNi-MOF with $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the CA bridge improves the electrical conductivity of the FeNi- $\mathsf{M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}}$ heterocatalyst, which can boost the electron transfer characteristic of the heterocatalyst during OER. The optical band-gap width of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ , FeNi-MOF, and $\mathsf{\bar{\ e N i-M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}-2}}$ was estimated utilizing Tauc’s equation to further understand the electronic interaction on the catalysts’ electronic property. The optical band-gap width of FeNi-MOF $\mathsf{C A}/$ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–2$ is $3.23\ \mathrm{\eV},$ , which is lower than $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(0\mathsf{H})_{2}$ $(3.63\ \mathrm{eV})$ and FeNi-MOF $(3.41~\mathrm{eV})$ , as depicted in Fig. S11. Therefore, the electronic interaction between FeNi-MOF and $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ reduces the band-gap of the $\mathrm{FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO_{4}{-}2}$ heterocatalysts, enhancing their charge transfer capability which is consistent with the DFT calculations results (Fig. 4e). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed to provides more insight into the effect of electronic interaction on the charge transfer property of the heterocatalysts. The Nyquist plots, as well as the equivalent circuits (Fig. 5d), demonstrate that the charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1, FeNi-MOF/CA/ $N i\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{-}_{2}$ 2, and $\mathsf{\bar{\ e l l i-M O F/C A/N i M o O_{4}-3}}$ are 2.45, 1.64, and $3.15\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, smaller than those of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ $(6.26~\Omega~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ), $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ $(8.45\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and FeNi-MOF $\mathrm{'}4.86\ \Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2\cdot}$ ). Therefore, the electron transfer from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the CA bridge enhances conductivity and reduces $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ values, which are responsible for the improved OER activities of the heterocatalysts. Besides, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}},$ electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) is a pivotal factor for the OER activity of an electrocatalyst [57,58]. Therefore, we evaluated the ECSAs of the catalysts through the double-layer capacitances $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ based on the CV curves at various scan rates (Fig. S12). As demonstrated in Fig. 5e, the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values of FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-1, FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–2$ , and $\mathrm{FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO_{4}{-}3}$ are 20.7, 25.6, and 19.6 mF $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, larger than those of $\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ $(8.29\mathrm{\mF\cm^{-2}}.$ ), $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ $\mathrm{^{15.6~mF~cm^{-2}}},$ , and FeNi-MOF $(17.5~\mathrm{\mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ). Therefore, coupling FeNi-MOF with $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the CA bridge can provide more accessible active sites for OER [59]. Even after the LSV curves are normalized by ECSA, the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 catalysts still exhibit higher OER activities than the uncoupled catalysts, as displayed in Fig. S13, implying that they possess higher intrinsic activities. Turnover frequency (TOF) data validates the increased intrinsic activities of heterocatalysts. As illustrated in Fig. S14, the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4 heterocatalysts exhibit lower overpotentials for achieving specific TOFs than $\mathsf{C A/N i M o O_{4}@N i(O H)_{2}}$ , $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ , and FeNi-MOF.
Apart from the activity, the durability of an electrocatalyst is also crucial for its practical energy-related applications. CV cycling tests and chronopotentiometry curves were used to assess the stability of the prepared FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2. The LSV curves of the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 exhibit a slight decrease in OER performance after 5000 continuous CV cycles, as illustrated in Fig. 6c. Furthermore, the current retention of the FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}–2$ after $50\mathrm{h}$ of electrolysis is $95\%$ at $280\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential, demonstrating its high stability and durability for OER (Fig. 6d). The post-OER FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 was characterized further with SEM, XRD, and XPS. The SEM image demonstrates that the post-OER FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 on the CC substrate retains its original 3D open structure, with negligible change in the spindlelike morphology of the FeNi-MOF nanorods (Fig. 6e). XRD pattern of the post-OER FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 indicates that the diffraction peaks of the FeNi-MOF are obvious, indicating their robust stability toward OER (Fig. 6f). However, the diffraction peaks intensity of $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ (JCPDS: 45-0142) was reduced after the test, and two diffraction peaks at $33.5^{\circ}$ and $53.4^{\circ}$ were observed, which can be indexed to ${\mathrm{Ni}}(0{\mathrm{H}})_{2}{\cdot}0.75{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ (JCPDS card number, 38-0175) and NiOOH (JCPDS card number, 27-0956), and is consistent with previous results [47]. The XPS survey spectra of FeNi-MOF/CA/ $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4^{-2}}$ before and after the stability test are compared in Fig. S15a, revealing that the elements Ni, Fe, Mo, C, and O are still present after the OER process. When compared to the pristine FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2, the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ peaks shift to higher binding energy (Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $712.0\mathrm{eV}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at $725.6\mathrm{eV}.$ , as illustrated in Fig. S15b. In the Ni 2p spectra, additional peaks at 857.1 and $875.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are observed after the OER (Fig. S15c), indicating the formation of NiOOH [45]. A TEM image of the post-OER FeNi-MOF is displayed in Fig. S16a. It can be clearly observed that the structure of the MOF is well-preserved, as evidenced by the elemental mappings of the MOF (Fig. S16c). Notably, ${\sim}10~\mathrm{nm}$ clusters appear on the surface of the FeNi-MOF. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images demonstrate that the interlayer distances of the clusters are $0.169\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.268~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which are well indexed to the (211) plane of NiOOH and (101) plane of ${\mathrm{Ni}}(0{\mathrm{H}})_{2}{\cdot}0.75{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0.$ , respectively (Fig. S16b). The TEM observations are consistent with the XPS results. Despite the formation of the clusters on the surface of
FeNi-MOF, the main electrocatalytic component, FeNi-MOF remained persistent leading to the robust stability of the selfsupported electrodes toward OER.
The Faradaic efficiency of the FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 was determined employing a two-electrode system (H-type electrolytic cell) in a 1.0 M KOH solution. $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generated in the electrolytic cell was collected by the water drainage method (Fig. S17a). The electrolyte was separated by the Nafion membrane, and the bubbles produced during the electrolysis can be clearly seen on the electrode surfaces (Fig. S17 b, c). Additionally, the variation in the dial of the measuring cylinder can be observed after every $5~\mathrm{min}$ of electrolysis (Fig. S17 d, e). Fig. S17f depicts the timedependent amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ driven by the FeNi- $10\mathrm{F/CA/NiMoO_{4}}.$ - 2 electrode for $25{\mathrm{~min}}$ of electrolysis. The amounts of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ obtained are consistent with the theoretically calculated amounts (Fig. S18). Furthermore, the purity of $0_{2}$ generated in the electrolytic cell was determined to be $100\%$ using Gas Chromatography (GC) (Fig. S19), demonstrating that the self-supported electrode exhibits a Faradaic efficiency of about $100\%$ for oxygen evolution. Therefore, FeNi-MOF/CA/NiMoO4-2 is indeed an efficient and durable electrocatalyst towards OER.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we developed a simple approach for covalently bonding FeNi-MOF with $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ through the bridging role of CA on conductive CC substrate and observed that the CA is critical for the development of the organic-inorganic heterocatalyst. Notably, CA functioned as a charge transfer channel, enabling electrons to transfer from FeNi-MOF to $\mathsf{N i M o O}_{4}$ . The charge transfer modified the electronic structure of the heterocatalyst, reduced the charge transfer resistance, and enhanced the adsorption capacity of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ intermediates on MOFs, thereby improving the heterocatalyst’s OER activity. The optimized heterostructure demonstrated superior OER performance with an overpotential of only $256~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , outperforming the benchmark $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ catalyst and the reported catalysts [15,19,60–63]. This work provides an efficient strategy for the production of oxygen and value-added compounds in the field of energy conversion and storage by developing an excellent approach for the fabrication of MOFs-based heterocatalysts with enhanced intrinsic OER activity.
